---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/selection-framework';
const title = "Shopify Email App Selection Framework — Operator Playbook";
const desc = description(site, 'guides/selection-framework');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which Shopify email app wins this framework?",
    "answer": "The option that meets the store’s mandatory requirements with adequate evidence and an acceptable cost and operating burden. The framework does not preassign a brand winner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a staff-account trial prove revenue lift?",
    "answer": "No. It can verify scoped logic and operator tasks. Production performance and causal business outcomes require separate observations and suitable measurement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a high score offset a missing requirement?",
    "answer": "A mandatory requirement is a separate gate. Preferences can be weighted only after mandatory failures and unresolved conditions are made explicit."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
      <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Selection Framework</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Select an email app against requirements your team can verify</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Start with the workflows, data, costs and operating responsibilities the store needs. Compare the current stack with alternatives using the same evidence. This framework produces a decision record and an implementation handoff, not a universal app winner or a claim that a trial has already been run.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Define the decision and the incumbent baseline</h2>
      <p>State what is failing today and what would count as a useful change: unreliable suppression, an unsupported data field, slow operator edits or an unaffordable forecast. Record the current workflow, workaround, recurring cost and who maintains it. Include repairing the incumbent as a candidate so switching is not assumed to be the only solution.</p>
      <p>Distinguish a primary lifecycle platform from a capture tool, SMS layer or reporting service. Compare complete operating stacks with explicit responsibilities. A shortlist size follows evaluation capacity and meaningful alternatives; it is not a universal limit on how many suppliers a store may use.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Write requirements as observable behavior</h2>
      <p>For each requirement name the trigger, source data, audience, timing, suppression, action, fallback and output evidence. Example exercise: a qualifying cart enters a reminder journey, a purchase arrives before dispatch, and the pending reminder must be cancelled or excluded. This is a proposed acceptance case, not evidence that any named product passes it.</p>
      <p>Classify each requirement as mandatory, preferred or deferred and record why. Set the acceptance condition before vendor demonstrations. A mandatory permission or queue-control requirement cannot be offset by attractive templates or a low fee. Define any accepted workaround with an owner, cost and failure response.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Cover six operating dimensions</h2>
      <p>Use lifecycle fit, required Shopify data, cost, operator capacity, channel coordination and migration risk as review areas. Under each, list the specific requirements for this store. Data depth means the exact fields and freshness needed, not the largest advertised event catalog. Channel coordination means shared identity, suppression and journey ownership across the actual stack.</p>
      <p>Operator capacity includes who can change a rule, review it, diagnose a failed sync and restore a previous version. Migration risk includes transferable permission evidence, historical events, in-flight journeys and archive access. Avoid assigning points by brand or using order-count thresholds as proxies for these requirements.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Collect current evidence with scope</h2>
      <p>For each finalist, retain dated primary documentation, the plan and integration version it describes, a written quote and any supplier clarification. Mark a capability documented, demonstrated, verified in the scoped exercise, unavailable or unresolved. These statuses are different forms of evidence, not interchangeable marketing checkmarks.</p>
      <p>Record region, channel, account permissions and plan restrictions. A feature shown by a supplier on a different plan does not prove availability in the quoted package. If a critical behavior cannot be verified, carry that gap into the decision rather than inventing a capability or assuming a planned integration already works.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Run the same bounded workflow exercise</h2>
      <p>Prepare representative non-sending fixtures: a guest customer, variant or product context, a permission change, a duplicate event and a purchase arriving before a pending reminder. Let the future operator configure the workflow and trace each fixture. Record prerequisites, assistance, actual results and unresolved failures for each candidate.</p>
      <p>Include editing, debugging, pausing and restoring the workflow. Set time and support expectations from the team’s real needs, not a universal half-hour rule. Staff fixtures can test logic and usability; they do not establish production deliverability, peak load performance, merchant recovery rate or revenue lift.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Verify data boundaries and failure recovery</h2>
      <p>Trace a source field from its origin to audience membership, message rendering and reporting. Check missing values, stale inventory or subscription state, event retries, permission updates and delayed orders. Ask how operators see sync failures and how they prevent stale actions while recovering. Require evidence for the workflow’s actual dependencies.</p>
      <p>For a multi-vendor stack, identify the authority for suppression and which system stops competing sends. Verify that a pause affects the intended queue, not just new entrants. Assign responsibility for each boundary; a collection of individually capable tools can still fail if no system owns the handoff.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Compare total cost under the same workload</h2>
      <p>Use the pricing-models worksheet for stored contacts, subscribed identities, send volume, peak usage, seats, add-ons and migration labor. Apply each written quote to the same scenarios. Show unresolved billing units and feature gates. Include ongoing workarounds and retained subscriptions rather than comparing entry prices alone.</p>
      <p>Separate cost savings from speculative revenue improvement. Avoid a generic payback multiple as an acceptance rule. Finance can choose a budget and risk tolerance appropriate to the decision; forecast assumptions and sensitivity should remain visible when a small change in usage changes the cheapest candidate.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Use scoring only after mandatory gates</h2>
      <p>First list pass, fail and unresolved mandatory requirements with evidence. Then compare preferences using weights agreed before seeing totals. Define what each rating means in observable terms, such as verified self-service completion versus a documented manual workaround. Do not convert missing evidence into a neutral passing score.</p>
      <p>Test whether reasonable changes in weights or workload reverse the ordering. If they do, explain the deciding tradeoff and uncertainty. A total is a decision aid, not proof of universal superiority. The chosen option may be the incumbent if the alternative’s benefit does not justify transition cost and operational risk.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Resolve the decision with an explicit record</h2>
      <p>Document the selected stack, rejected alternatives, decisive requirements, accepted limitations, recurring cost forecast, transition cost and evidence links. Assign owners and dates for unresolved conditions that must close before activation. If a mandatory gate remains unresolved, the record should say that selection is conditional, not describe the option as fully validated.</p>
      <p>Use the migration guide to plan pause-before-enable ownership, permission transfer and rollback reconciliation. Selection does not itself authorize sending or prove a safe cutover. Choose the release window from operational readiness, trading commitments and recovery capacity rather than banning entire calendar quarters.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Hand off and define when to reconsider</h2>
      <p>Give implementation the workflow specification, fixture results, field contracts, cost model, access requirements, known gaps and responsible owners. Preserve the evidence version so later changes can be assessed against what was selected. Teach the actual operator how to diagnose the tested failure cases rather than leaving only a vendor recommendation.</p>
      <p>Reopen the decision when a material requirement, contract, catalog, channel or team responsibility changes. Do not force a switch simply because a volume threshold is crossed. Compare real post-launch observations with the original acceptance conditions and keep measured outcomes separate from the expected benefits recorded during selection.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><nav aria-label="Related operator guides" class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
    <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Quote and workload comparison →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/implementation-plan">Implementation handoff →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Transition planning →</a>
  </nav></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Selection framework FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
